# Dashboard Design System — Cursor Rule

This project is a production operational dashboard.

The visual system is defined by these source-of-truth documents:

- `docs/design/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`
- `docs/design/UI_ART_DIRECTION.md`
- `docs/design/CURSOR_IMPLEMENTATION_PROMPT.md`

Read and follow them before making substantial UI changes.

## 1. Primary objective

Build a functional operational interface with a distinctive visual identity inspired by:

- Modern Neo-Brutalism
- Neo-Memphis
- Geometric editorial design
- Bold typography
- Cream/off-white surfaces
- Deep navy structural elements
- Lime, orange, and blue accents
- Modular asymmetric composition
- Controlled geometric decoration
- Subtle ambient motion

Do NOT copy the reference image's exact layout or content.

## 2. Functional priority

Always preserve this hierarchy:

1. Operational information
2. User actions
3. Navigation
4. Status and alerts
5. Data visualization
6. Decorative visual language

Never allow decoration or animation to reduce usability.

Do not modify business logic, APIs, authentication, routing, state management, or existing functionality unless the task explicitly requires it.

## 3. Inspect before implementing

Before creating or modifying UI:

- Inspect the existing project structure.
- Identify the frontend framework and styling approach.
- Search for existing reusable components.
- Search for existing design tokens/theme variables.
- Reuse existing layout primitives.
- Avoid duplicate components.
- Follow established project naming, file, TypeScript, linting, formatting, and testing conventions.

Do not introduce a new UI library or styling framework unless there is a clear project-level reason.

## 4. Design tokens are mandatory

Prefer design tokens/CSS variables/theme values over arbitrary hardcoded visual values.

Use the values defined in `docs/design/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`.

Core palette:

- Background: `#F7F5EE`
- Surface: `#FFFEF9`
- Ink: `#111426`
- Lime: `#C5F238`
- Orange: `#FF6418`
- Blue: `#58A8F5`
- Yellow: `#F7C84B`

Do not invent unrelated colors without a design-system reason.

## 5. Component reuse

Prefer reusable variants instead of one-off styling.

Reuse or create consistent components for:

- AppShell
- Sidebar
- TopBar
- PageHeader
- Card
- KPI
- ChartCard
- TableCard
- ActivityFeed
- StatusCard
- AlertCard
- ActionCard
- Button
- Badge
- Tabs
- Search
- Filter
- Pagination
- EmptyState
- GeometricDecoration

Before creating a new component, search the codebase for an equivalent component.

## 6. Geometric decoration system

Decorative geometry is a reusable system, not manually scattered markup.

Supported primitives may include:

- square
- rectangle
- circle
- half-circle
- capsule
- arc
- outline-square
- outline-circle
- line
- dots
- grid
- cube
- extruded-cube
- geometric-fragment

A decoration should be configurable by:

- type
- size
- color
- rotation
- position
- opacity
- z-index
- animation
- delay

Do not hardcode decorative SVG/HTML fragments independently inside many pages.

## 7. Controlled randomization

Decorations should feel organic but remain deterministic.

DO NOT use uncontrolled `Math.random()` during React/render execution or equivalent rendering paths.

Prefer a stable seeded or deterministic selection based on component identity, predefined variants, or another stable mechanism.

The same component should not visibly reshuffle decorations on every render.

Decoration selection should consider:

- component type
- component importance
- available whitespace
- surrounding background
- nearby decoration patterns
- responsive breakpoint

Approximate distribution:

- 35% of cards: no decoration
- 40%: one decoration
- 20%: two decorations
- 5%: three-to-five decorations in large featured areas

Dense tables, forms, and operational data regions should usually have no decoration or only an extremely subtle accent.

## 8. Decoration placement

Safe locations include:

- top-left
- top-right
- bottom-left
- bottom-right
- card edges
- unused whitespace
- between sections
- near, but not over, visual content

Decorations may slightly cross card boundaries when compositionally appropriate.

Never overlap:

- important text
- KPI values
- buttons
- inputs
- tables
- chart labels
- navigation
- alerts
- interactive targets

Use z-index and overflow intentionally.

## 9. Visual variation

Do not repeat the same decorative pattern in adjacent cards.

Avoid sequences such as:

- same cube
- same position
- same color
- same rotation

Instead vary:

- geometry
- size
- color
- position
- rotation
- animation
- density

Variation must remain within the design system.

## 10. Animation

Decorative animation is ambient and subtle.

Allowed variants:

- float
- drift
- rotate
- pulse
- orbit
- micro-shift

Typical duration:

- 4–12 seconds

Use different delays and durations so objects do not move synchronously.

Avoid:

- bouncing
- flashing
- fast movement
- large scale changes
- aggressive parallax
- attention-seeking motion

Interactive UI transitions should generally be around 150–600ms.

## 11. Reduced motion

Always support:

`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`

When reduced motion is requested:

- disable floating
- disable rotation
- disable orbit
- minimize unnecessary transitions

The visual composition should remain valid without animation.

## 12. Responsive decoration

Desktop:
- full decoration system
- asymmetric composition
- subtle overlaps

Tablet:
- approximately 60–70% of desktop decoration density
- simpler overlaps

Mobile:
- approximately 25–40% of desktop decoration density
- smaller objects
- reduced animation
- no risky overlaps
- no horizontal overflow

Functional content always takes priority.

## 13. Cards

Cards should share the same design language but do not need identical compositions.

Supported conceptual variants:

- default
- minimal
- lime
- orange
- blue
- dark
- featured

Use:

- strong structural borders
- moderate radius
- controlled spacing
- limited shadows
- clear hierarchy

Avoid generic SaaS cards with excessive blur, glass, gradients, or shadows.

## 14. Typography

Use the established display/UI font system.

Display typography should be:

- bold
- geometric
- highly legible

Use large, confident numerical typography for KPIs.

Functional text, tables, forms, and metadata must remain highly readable.

Do not sacrifice readability for stylistic typography.

## 15. Data visualization

Charts are operational components.

Prioritize:

- readability
- clear labels
- strong hierarchy
- limited colors
- minimal grid lines
- responsive behavior

Do not place distracting decoration inside chart plotting areas.

## 16. Tables

Tables must remain calm and readable.

Use:

- strong headers
- clear columns
- row hover
- status badges
- sorting/filtering where existing functionality supports them
- accessible focus states

Do not place decorative objects over dense data.

## 17. Interaction states

Every interactive component should have appropriate:

- default
- hover
- active
- focus
- disabled
- loading
- success/error states where applicable

Interaction feedback should feel tactile and geometric, using subtle translation, border changes, or hard-shadow shifts when appropriate.

## 18. Accessibility

Preserve or improve:

- keyboard navigation
- visible focus
- semantic controls
- adequate contrast
- accessible labels
- non-color-only status communication
- reduced-motion support

Decorative elements must never appear to be interactive controls.

## 19. No arbitrary visual drift

Do not introduce:

- random new colors
- unrelated gradients
- unrelated shadows
- inconsistent border widths
- inconsistent corner radii
- unrelated icon styles
- unrelated animation styles

If a new visual pattern is genuinely needed, first consider whether it belongs as a reusable design-system token or component variant.

## 20. Implementation workflow

For a new screen:

1. Inspect existing implementation.
2. Identify the operational hierarchy.
3. Reuse the design tokens.
4. Reuse existing components.
5. Build the structural layout.
6. Establish typography and color hierarchy.
7. Add data visualization and functional controls.
8. Add geometric decoration only after the functional composition works.
9. Apply controlled deterministic decoration.
10. Add subtle ambient animation.
11. Validate responsive behavior.
12. Validate accessibility.
13. Run lint/typecheck/tests available in the project.
14. Review for visual consistency with `docs/design/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md` and `docs/design/UI_ART_DIRECTION.md`.

## 21. Review standard

Before considering a UI task complete, verify:

- Does the page remain operationally usable?
- Is the primary information immediately understandable?
- Are important KPIs dominant?
- Are tables and charts readable?
- Are interactions obvious?
- Are decorations secondary?
- Is randomness controlled and stable?
- Are animations subtle?
- Does the design work without animation?
- Does the page work on mobile?
- Is there no horizontal overflow?
- Does the page use existing tokens/components?
- Does the page look like part of the same product?

## Golden rule

Design the dashboard first. Use decoration to strengthen the composition afterward.

The geometric system is a supporting visual language, never the content itself.
